        <h2>Why Liverpool Homes Are Particularly at Risk</h2>
        <p>Liverpool does get real cold snaps, most often in January and February. Many Victorian and Edwardian homes have pipes in cold spots. Think bare loft pipes, cellars, back extensions and outside walls. These homes were not built with modern insulation.</p>
        <p>A frozen pipe does not always burst straight away. The damage often comes when it thaws. The weak part of the pipe splits under pressure. A home that looks fine on a freezing night can have water coming through the ceiling the next morning.</p>

        <div class="blog-stat">
          <div class="stat-num">13°C</div>
          <div class="stat-label">The lowest you should set your heating when you go away in cold weather. Aim for 13 to 15 degrees, rather than turning it off.</div>
        </div>
        <CallStrip inline title="Got a plumbing problem now?" text="A real person answers 24/7. No call-out fee." />

        <h2>Which Liverpool Properties Are Most at Risk?</h2>
        <p>Some homes are at more risk than others. Watch out for:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Victorian and Edwardian terraces with bare pipes in the loft, or water tanks in the cellar</li>
          <li>Pipes that run through cold extensions, garages or sheds</li>
          <li>Pipes in cellar entries</li>
          <li>Homes left without heat for a long time, such as holiday homes or lets between tenants</li>
          <li>Very old copper pipes that have been weakened by frost before</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to Prevent Frozen Pipes in Liverpool</h2>

        <h3>Insulate exposed pipes before winter</h3>
        <p>Wrap pipes in lofts, garages, cellar entries and on outside walls with foam pipe lagging. It is cheap from any DIY shop and easy to fit. If you have a cold water tank in the loft, wrap that too. Cover the top and sides but not the bottom. That lets warmth from the house reach it.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/insulation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Energy Saving Trust</a> has more advice on insulating your home.</p>

        <h3>Keep the heating on a low background temperature</h3>
        <p>If you go away for more than a day in cold weather, do not turn the heating off. Set it to keep the house at 13 to 15 degrees. That costs far less than fixing a burst pipe and the water damage it causes.</p>

        <h3>Know where your stopcock is</h3>
        <p>When a pipe bursts, every second counts. Everyone in the house should know where the stopcock is. In Victorian terraces it is often under the kitchen sink, in the cellar or near the front door. If you have not used it for a while, check it turns freely before winter.</p>

        <h3>Drip your taps in a severe cold snap</h3>
        <p>If it is set to drop below -5 degrees, leave a tap dripping overnight. This keeps water moving through the pipes. Moving water is less likely to freeze than still water.</p>

        <h2>How to Spot a Frozen Pipe Before It Bursts</h2>
        <p>The key time is after a pipe freezes but before it bursts. Catch it then, and you can stop the damage.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>No water from a tap, even though the mains is on</li>
          <li>Less water from some taps but not others</li>
          <li>Frost on a bare pipe</li>
          <li>A pipe that bulges or looks discoloured</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What to Do if You Suspect a Frozen Pipe</h2>
        <p>Call us on <a href="tel:01515580334">0151 558 0334</a> straight away. Never use a naked flame or boiling water to thaw a pipe. These do more harm than the frost.</p>
        <p>If you can reach the pipe and want to try, use a hairdryer on a warm, not hot, setting. Start at the tap end and work back slowly towards the frozen part. Stop at once if you see a crack or water seeping from a joint.</p>
        <p>Turn off the stopcock first, even if the pipe has not burst. Then if it splits as it thaws, the water is already off.</p>

        <h2>What to Do if the Pipe Has Already Burst</h2>
        <p>Follow the same steps as for any burst pipe:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Turn off the water at the stopcock straight away</li>
          <li>Turn off the boiler and any immersion heater</li>
          <li>Open all cold taps to drain the system</li>
          <li>Move valuables away from the water and take photos of the damage</li>
          <li>Call us on <a href="tel:01515580334">0151 558 0334</a>. We answer 24/7 across Liverpool</li>
          <li>Call your insurer once the water is under control</li>
        </ol>
        <CallStrip inline title="Want us to take a look?" text="Call for advice or a price. Gas Safe registered, no call-out fee." />

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="pf-faq">
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-red">EMERGENCY</span><span class="pf-faq-q">What should I do if I think a pipe has frozen?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Turn off the stopcock to be safe. Never use a naked flame to thaw the pipe. Call us on <a href="tel:01515580334">0151 558 0334</a> straight away. A frozen pipe caught before it bursts is a small job. A burst pipe can cause water damage that costs thousands. Acting fast is always the right call.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">PREVENTION</span><span class="pf-faq-q">What is the most important thing I can do to prevent frozen pipes?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Lag bare pipes in cold spaces before winter. Pipes in lofts, cellar entries and cold extensions are most at risk in older Liverpool homes. Foam lagging is cheap and easy to fit. When you go away, keep the heating on low too.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">TIMING</span><span class="pf-faq-q">When are Liverpool pipes most at risk of freezing?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Cold snaps are most common in January and February. The risk is highest when it stays below freezing for more than a day, most of all after a sudden drop from mild weather. Homes left cold over Christmas and New Year are at high risk.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">INSURANCE</span><span class="pf-faq-q">Does home insurance cover burst pipes from freezing?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Most home insurance covers water damage from burst pipes, including frozen ones. Check your excess, and whether trace and access is included. We can write a leak report for your insurer. Take photos of all the damage before you clean up.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </div>

